## Solution

We claim $I(\mathbb{Z}^n) = \{0\}$, i.e., the only polynomial in $\mathbb{C}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ vanishing on every point of $\mathbb{Z}^n$ is the zero polynomial.

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the definition of $I(S)$ and the question. No solution, answer, or proof sketch is embedded. Proceeding.

**Proof.** We proceed by induction on $n$.

**Base case ($n=1$).** Let $f \in \mathbb{C}[x_1]$ vanish on all of $\mathbb{Z}$. Since $\mathbb{Z}$ is infinite, $f$ has infinitely many roots. A nonzero univariate polynomial over a field has at most $\deg f$ roots, so $f$ must be the zero polynomial.

**Inductive step.** Assume the result holds for $n-1$ (with $n \geq 2$). Let $f \in \mathbb{C}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ vanish on $\mathbb{Z}^n$. View $f$ as a polynomial in $x_n$ with coefficients in $\mathbb{C}[x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}]$:

$$f = \sum_{j=0}^{d} g_j(x_1,\dots,x_{n-1})\, x_n^{j}, \qquad g_j \in \mathbb{C}[x_1,\dots,x_{n-1}].$$

Fix any point $(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}) \in \mathbb{Z}^{n-1}$. Then the univariate polynomial

$$h(x_n) := f(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}, x_n) = \sum_{j=0}^{d} g_j(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1})\, x_n^{j} \in \mathbb{C}[x_n]$$

vanishes on every integer $a_n \in \mathbb{Z}$ (since $(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1},a_n) \in \mathbb{Z}^n$). By the base case, $h \equiv 0$, so every coefficient vanishes:

$$g_j(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}) = 0 \quad \text{for all } j = 0,\dots,d.$$

Since this holds for **every** $(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}) \in \mathbb{Z}^{n-1}$, each $g_j$ vanishes on all of $\mathbb{Z}^{n-1}$. By the inductive hypothesis, $g_j = 0$ for all $j$. Therefore $f = 0$.

By induction, $I(\mathbb{Z}^n) = \{0\}$ for all $n \geq 1$. $\blacksquare$

